# bigo kata profile, SPACE axis. Companion to kata.assume, loaded by -kata.
#
# A SEPARATE FILE ON PURPOSE. An entry in kata.assume claims what a call
# COSTS; an entry here claims what it ALLOCATES. Letting one line assert both
# would make half of every entry a claim nobody reasoned about, which is the
# shape behind most of this project's wrong bounds. Writing them apart forces
# the second question to be asked out loud — and the answers genuinely differ:
# several entries below are constant on ANY model, while others are constant
# only because K-1 says input is not graded. That distinction is marked.
#
# Space here is HEAP. An external callee has no body to analyze, so without
# an entry every stdlib call is unverifiable heap.

# --- constant on ANY model: these allocate nothing at all ----------------
strconv.Atoi O(1)
# Returns an int. No allocation, K-1 or not.
strings.Compare O(1)
# Returns an int. Reads both operands, allocates nothing.
cmp.Compare O(1)
# Returns an int.
math/rand.Intn O(1)
# Returns an int.
(*bufio.Scanner).Buffer O(1)
# Takes a caller-supplied buffer and stores it; allocates nothing itself.
(*bufio.Writer).WriteString O(1)
# Copies into the existing buffer, flushing when full. No growth.
(*bufio.Writer).Flush O(1)
# Drains the existing buffer.

# --- constant because of K-1: these DO allocate, and it is input ---------
bufio.NewScanner O(1)
# Allocates a Scanner. Its buffer is sized by the input it will read, and
# under K-1 the input is not part of what is graded.
bufio.NewWriter O(1)
# Allocates a fixed 4096-byte buffer, so constant in the program's sizes.
(*bufio.Scanner).Scan O(1)
# May grow the token buffer up to its configured maximum. That maximum is a
# property of the input, not of the algorithm.
(*bufio.Scanner).Text O(1)
# Allocates a string copy of the token just read. Linear in that token, which
# is input.
strings.Split O(1)
# Allocates a slice of substrings, linear in the input line being split.
strings.Fields O(1)
# The other common way the same input line gets split.

# --- constant on ANY model: the hashing path -----------------------------
(hash.Hash32).Sum32 O(1)
# Returns a uint32. Allocates nothing, K-1/K-2 or not.
(io.Writer).Write O(1)
# Writes into storage the writer already holds — a bufio buffer, a hash's
# accumulator. Growth, where a writer grows at all, is bounded by that
# writer's own configured capacity and not by a size in the program.
hash/fnv.New32 O(1)
# Allocates a sum32: one 32-bit word. The companion time entries are CURATED
# rather than profile entries, so the coverage invariant does not demand these
# — they are here because a call priced O(1) for time and left ⊤ for space
# leaves the row unverifiable anyway, which is the half-fix the two-file split
# exists to make visible.
hash/fnv.New32a O(1)
hash/fnv.New64 O(1)
hash/fnv.New64a O(1)
hash/fnv.New128 O(1)
hash/fnv.New128a O(1)
# One or two fixed-width words each; New128/New128a allocate a two-word struct.
strconv.Itoa O(1)
# Allocates the formatted string, which the curated TIME table already reasons
# is constant-bounded: at most 20 digits for an int64 in base 10. Not an fnv
# entry, but the last thing standing between a hashing Index and its O(1)
# space claim, and true on any model rather than only under K-1.
